# A FAILED WRITE IS NOT LOST (0.17.48, the audit). storefail OBJ N fails the next N writes of a flash object (plat.h: 5 lofi's pieces, 25 the bass
# steps). A piece's write that fails stays waiting (dirty); a genre switch with it waiting and failing again is refused (the genre stays, the
# piece in RAM); once a write works, the switch goes on and the piece is there when back. The bass steps' write failing keeps them waiting too
# (it cleared them first: the retry had nothing to write, and the piece came back with its old steps).
seed 0
wait 3000
press GLO
turn PRESET 1
release GLO
wait 300
expect genre 1
turn PRESET 2
wait 200
expect piece 2
tap ENV
spin K1 -10
wait 100
expect par0 44
storefail 5 2
tap GLO
tap SAVE
wait 500
expect dirty 1
# another genre: refused, nothing lost
press GLO
turn PRESET 1
release GLO
wait 300
expect genre 1
expect dirty 1
expect par0 44
# now the write works: the switch goes on; back, the stored piece
press GLO
turn PRESET 1
release GLO
wait 300
expect genre 2
expect dirty 0
press GLO
turn PRESET -1
release GLO
wait 300
expect genre 1
expect saved 4
expect par0 44
# the bass steps: a step set (Bass steps page), stored; their write fails once, then works
tap LFO
tap LFO
tap LFO
tap LFO
tap LFO
wait 300
expect view 10
tapkey 4
spin K1 -10
spin K1 2
wait 100
expect bskind2 2
storefail 25 1
tap GLO
tap SAVE
wait 500
expect dirty 1
tap PLAY
wait 3000
expect dirty 0
reboot
wait 3000
press GLO
turn PRESET 1
release GLO
wait 300
expect genre 1
turn PRESET 2
wait 300
expect bskind2 2
# the queue: commands posted faster than the engine takes them are counted (QD on the load line), not silently lost
flood 2000
wait 100
expect qdrops >0
expect playing 1
